@node Calculator Quick Start
@section Quick Start

@table @kbd
@item M-x cmacs-calculator
The landing page: every calculator this build has, grouped by category.
@key{RET} opens a sheet pre-filled with a runnable call.
@item C-c =
Evaluate the expression at point, @emph{in any major mode}.
@end table

@noindent
From a shell:

@example
$ ./src/emacs --calc "sqrt(5/3*3^4)"
11.6189500386
@end example

@node Calculator Semantics
@section The Corrected Semantics (Read This First)

@cindex calculator traps
@cindex calc precedence

Calc's defaults are tuned for its interactive RPN Calculator, not for a desktop
calculator.  Piping a string straight to @code{calc-eval} @strong{gives wrong
answers}.  This is the single most important thing to understand about this
subsystem: it exists because of five specific traps, each corrected in the
engine.  Every number below was produced by running both engines.

@subsection Trap 1: Multiplication Binds Tighter Than Division

In stock Calc @samp{*} has precedence over @samp{/}, so @samp{2/3*4} means
@samp{2/(3*4)}.  This is the trap most likely to silently cost you a real
answer:

@multitable @columnfractions .30 .35 .35
@headitem Expression @tab Stock GNU Calc @tab cmacs-calculator
@item @samp{2/3*4}
@tab @samp{0.166666666667} @tab @samp{2.66666666667}
@item @samp{sqrt(5/3*3^4)}
@tab @samp{0.143443827637} @tab @samp{11.6189500386}
@end multitable

Every pocket calculator, spreadsheet and programming language reads
@samp{(2/3)*4 = 2.667}.  cmacs pins @code{calc-multiplication-has-precedence} to
@code{nil} so @samp{/} and @samp{*} associate left to right.

The fix cannot come from Calc's @samp{c} language mode, which would also correct
the precedence but redefines @samp{^} as bitwise XOR --- @samp{2^10} would stop
meaning 1024.  @code{calc-language} is therefore pinned to @code{nil}.

@subsection Trap 2: Angles Default to Degrees

Stock Calc evaluates @samp{sin(90)} to @samp{1}.  cmacs defaults
@code{calc-angle-mode} to @code{rad}, which is what physics and calculus assume
--- in degree mode, d/dx sin(x) quietly picks up a pi/180 factor.

@multitable @columnfractions .30 .35 .35
@headitem Expression @tab Stock GNU Calc @tab cmacs-calculator
@item @samp{sin(90)}   @tab @samp{1}             @tab @samp{0.8939966636}
@item @samp{sin(pi/2)} @tab @samp{sin(pi / 2)}   @tab @samp{1.}
@end multitable

Switch with @code{cmacs-calculator-angle-mode} (@code{rad} / @code{deg} /
@code{hms}), or per session in the REPL with @samp{:deg} and @samp{:rad}.

@subsection Trap 3: Modes Must Be Passed, Not @code{let}-Bound

@code{calc-eval} @strong{re-binds the mode variables itself}, so an enclosing
@code{let} over @code{calc-angle-mode} is silently ignored.  The only mechanism
Calc honours is @code{calc-eval}'s documented list form:

@lisp
(calc-eval (list EXPR 'calc-angle-mode 'rad 'calc-internal-prec 12 ...))
@end lisp

@noindent
which is what @code{cmacs-calculator--modes} builds.  (Org does the same thing
with @code{org-calc-default-modes}.)  If you extend the subsystem, pass modes
through the @var{MODES} argument of @code{cmacs-calculator-eval} --- never wrap
a @code{let} around it and expect it to take.

@strong{Consequence worth knowing:} that list form sets the mode variables
@emph{globally and permanently} --- stock Calc behaviour, not a cmacs choice.
Once a calculator expression has been evaluated in a session, a subsequent plain
@kbd{M-x calc} or bare @code{calc-eval} in that session inherits the corrected
modes.  For most people that is an improvement; it is documented so it is never
a surprise.

@subsection Trap 4: Symbolic Constants Do Not Fold

Stock Calc leaves @samp{sin(pi/2)} as @samp{sin(pi / 2)} and @samp{e^(i*pi)} as
@samp{e^(i pi)} --- mathematically honest, useless on a calculator.  cmacs wraps
every numeric evaluation in Calc's @code{evalv()}, which forces evaluation:

@multitable @columnfractions .30 .35 .35
@headitem Expression @tab Stock GNU Calc @tab cmacs-calculator
@item @samp{e^(i*pi)} @tab @samp{e^(i pi)} @tab @samp{-1.}
@end multitable

@code{evalv} is safe for symbolic work too: @samp{evalv(deriv(x^3,x))} is still
@samp{3 x^2}.

@subsection Trap 5: Bad Input Comes Back Unevaluated

This is the footgun.  Calc reports bad input by @emph{returning it} --- you
cannot tell a rejected call from a result:

@multitable @columnfractions .20 .28 .52
@headitem Expression @tab Stock GNU Calc @tab cmacs-calculator
@item @samp{1/0}    @tab @samp{1/0}    @tab @samp{uinf}
@item @samp{0/0}    @tab @samp{0/0}    @tab @samp{nan}
@item @samp{ln(0)}  @tab @samp{ln(0)}  @tab @samp{-inf}
@item @samp{foo(1)} @tab @samp{foo(1)} @tab signals @i{Unknown function: foo}
@item @samp{((1+2)} @tab @samp{3} (!)  @tab signals @i{unbalanced parentheses}
@end multitable

Note @samp{((1+2)} especially: Calc's parser stops at the first complete
expression and @emph{silently ignores the rest}, so a typo produces a plausible
answer.  cmacs corrects this three ways:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{calc-infinite-mode} turns division by zero into @samp{uinf} / @samp{nan} /
@samp{-inf} (set @code{cmacs-calculator-infinite-mode} to @code{nil} to signal).
@item
Validation rejects unbalanced delimiters, unknown functions and unbound
variables @emph{before} evaluation.
@item
The @emph{result} is checked too.  A domain rejection also comes back
unevaluated (@samp{lorentz(1.5)} @result{} @samp{"lorentz(1.5)"}, exactly as
stock Calc's own @samp{pmt(0,5,100)} does), which input validation cannot catch
--- the function is perfectly well-formed.  So numeric evaluation inspects the
output as well and signals rather than handing you a string that looks like a
result.
@end itemize

Set @code{cmacs-calculator-strict} to @code{nil} to restore Calc's
permissiveness.  Finally, @code{calc-eval} @emph{signals nothing on error
either}: it returns a @code{(POSITION MESSAGE)} list, easily mistaken for a
result.  @code{cmacs-calculator-eval} normalises that into a real
@code{cmacs-calculator-error} signal.

@node Calculator Surfaces
@section Usage Surfaces

@subsection The Sheet Buffer (@file{.calc})

The primary surface: a plain-text buffer of expressions, one per line, that
annotates itself with results.  Nothing here is a widget --- the file is text,
so it diffs, greps and travels over email.

@example
# a mortgage
price := 400000  @result{} 400000
loanpmt(price * 0.8, 0.065, 30)  @result{} 2022.61767478
@end example

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-c
Evaluate the whole buffer.
@item C-c C-e
Evaluate the current line.
@item C-c C-k
Strip every result annotation.
@item C-c C-p
Plot the expression on the line.
@item C-c C-s
Toggle symbolic (CAS) mode.
@end table

Results are @strong{idempotent}: each evaluation removes the previous annotation
before adding one, so evaluating ten times leaves one result per line --- which
is what makes eval-on-idle safe to run continuously.  A failing line annotates as
@samp{@result{} error: MESSAGE} and evaluation @emph{carries on}: one bad line
must not cost you the other forty.

@samp{NAME := EXPR} stores a value later lines can use.  Bindings are
@emph{sheet-local} (established with @code{cl-progv} around each evaluation), so
one sheet's variables never leak into another sheet or into the REPL.  Files
ending @file{.calc} open in @code{cmacs-calculator-sheet-mode} automatically.

@subsection The Landing Page

@kbd{M-x cmacs-calculator} renders @file{*cmacs-calculator*}: every registered
calculator grouped by category, with @key{RET} opening a sheet pre-filled with a
runnable call.  @kbd{/} or @kbd{s} filters, @kbd{n}/@kbd{p} (or @kbd{j}/@kbd{k})
move, @kbd{g} refreshes.

The listing is @emph{generated from the registry at render time} --- a family
added tomorrow appears with no edit, and an entry for something that no longer
exists is not expressible.  The header reports the @emph{active} chart tier
resolved through the chart module's own @code{auto} logic, so on a TTY it says
unicode rather than claiming SVG.

@subsection Inline Evaluation, in Any Major Mode

@kbd{C-c =} (@code{cmacs-calculator-eval-dwim}) evaluates the expression at
point wherever you are --- an Org note, a C comment, an email.  With an active
region, the region is the expression.

Otherwise the expression is the maximal run of calculator characters around
point.  Whitespace is deliberately not in that set, so ``the answer is sqrt(4)
here'' evaluates @samp{sqrt(4)} and not the sentence; but a run @emph{is}
extended across a gap when an operator sits on one side of it, so @samp{2 + 3}
still works.  Trailing sentence punctuation is trimmed only when it stops the
expression from parsing, so the decimal point in @samp{3.14} survives.

@subsection The @code{emacs --calc} CLI REPL

@example
emacs --calc            # interactive REPL (also reads piped stdin)
emacs --calc EXPR       # evaluate EXPR, print the result, exit
@end example

@noindent
A real session (piped stdin, verbatim):

@example
$ printf '2+2\nsqrt(2)\n:deg\nsin(90)\n:rad\nfoo(1)\n1/0\n' | emacs --calc
calc> 4
calc> 1.41421356237
calc> angle mode: degrees
calc> 1
calc> angle mode: radians
calc> error: Unknown function: foo
calc> uinf
@end example

Commands: @samp{:prec N}, @samp{:deg}, @samp{:rad}, @samp{:help}, @samp{:quit}
(@samp{:exit}, @samp{:q}).  A bad line never kills the loop --- every line is
evaluated inside a @code{condition-case} catching @code{error} wholesale,
because Calc reaches into a lot of code and a malformed expression can surface
as an arithmetic error from deep inside it.  Modes set in the REPL are
@emph{buffer-local}, so a REPL buffer inside a running Emacs cannot silently
reconfigure your other calculator surfaces.

@option{--calc} is implemented by @emph{rewriting argv} before Emacs parses it
(into @option{--batch} @option{--eval}), rather than by the @option{--bacon} /
@option{--crispy} pattern of taking over @code{main()}: those engines are C,
while Calc is Elisp, so the Lisp VM must be up before a single expression can be
evaluated.

@subsection MCP Tools

Registered when both @option{--with-cmacs-mcp} and
@option{--with-cmacs-calculator} are built.  All five are read-only.

@multitable @columnfractions .28 .72
@headitem Tool @tab Purpose
@item @code{calc_eval}
@tab Evaluate numerically, desktop semantics, arbitrary precision.
@item @code{calc_eval_symbolic}
@tab The CAS path: @code{deriv}, @code{integ}, @code{solve}, @code{taylor}.
@item @code{calc_convert_units}
@tab Convert to @code{units}, or reduce to base SI when omitted.
@item @code{calc_list}
@tab List registered calculators, optionally by @code{category}.
@item @code{calc_describe}
@tab Describe one calculator: title, args, returns, docs.
@end multitable

Every registered calculator is callable as a function from @code{calc_eval}, and
@code{calc_list} / @code{calc_describe} let an agent discover them without the
manual.

@node Calculator Families
@section Calculator Families

There are @strong{121 registered calculators}: 34 financial, 69 physics, 13
relativity and 5 tax.  Every one is defined with
@code{cmacs-calculator-defcalc}, which uses Calc's @code{defmath} --- so each is
a @emph{first-class algebraic function} that composes inside any larger
expression, anywhere an expression is legal:

@example
simpleint(1000,0.05,3) + 100                 @result{} 250.
bscall(100,100,0.05,0.2,1) - bsput(100,100,0.05,0.2,1)
                                             @result{} 4.8770575498
escapevel(5.972e24, 6.371e6) / 1000          @result{} 11.1859778919
@end example

One registry backs the landing page, completion, ElDoc, this documentation and
the MCP tool schema, so none of them can drift from the functions actually
defined.  @ref{Calculator Catalog} documents every one of the 121 calculators
--- what it does, the arguments it takes, and a worked example or two.  The
415 GNU Calc built-ins they compose with have a catalog of their own
(@pxref{Calculator Builtins}), and the constants both draw on are in
@ref{Calculator Constants}.

@subsection Financial

Calc already ships the time-value-of-money core in @file{calc-fin.el}
(@code{pv}, @code{fv}, @code{pmt}, @code{nper}, @code{rate}, @code{npv},
@code{irr}, @code{sln}, @code{syd}, @code{ddb}), and those are re-used as-is
rather than reimplemented.  This family adds what Calc lacks: amortization
schedules, bond pricing and risk measures, option pricing and greeks, and the
everyday ratios.

@example
simpleint(1000, 0.05, 3)                     @result{} 150.
compoundamt(1000, 0.05, 12, 10)              @result{} 1647.00949834
apr2apy(0.05, 12)                            @result{} 0.05116189792
cagr(1000, 2000, 10)                         @result{} 0.07177346254
loanpmt(300000, 0.065, 30)                   @result{} 1896.2040701
loantotal(300000, 0.065, 30)                 @result{} 682633.465236
bscall(100, 100, 0.05, 0.2, 1)               @result{} 10.4505835721
bsput(100, 100, 0.05, 0.2, 1)                @result{} 5.5735260223
@end example

Schedules and iterative solvers (amortization, yield-to-maturity, implied
volatility) return Lisp data or use Newton iteration, so they are plain
functions rather than algebraic ones.

@subsubsection Sign Conventions (Important)

@cindex sign convention
@cindex cash flow

Calc's @code{pv} / @code{fv} / @code{pmt} follow the @strong{signed cash-flow
convention}: money paid out is negative, money received is positive.  This
differs from the Excel / HP-12C reading some users expect:

@example
pv(0.05, 30, -1000)                          @result{} -15372.4510269
fv(0.05, 30, -1000)                          @result{} -66438.847503
pmt(0.05, 30, 100000)                        @result{} 6505.14350803
@end example

The cmacs wrappers (@code{loanpmt}, @code{loantotal}, @code{loanint}, @dots{})
instead return @emph{positive magnitudes}, which is what a loan calculator
should print.  The raw Calc functions remain available for anyone who wants the
signed convention --- both are correct, they just answer different questions.

@subsection Physics and Relativity

Two categories: @code{physics} (kinematics, dynamics, energy, momentum,
rotation, gravitation, oscillation, thermodynamics, electromagnetism, optics,
quantum) and @code{relativity} (special and general).

@strong{Units are SI, always.}  Every argument and every result is in base SI ---
metres, kilograms, seconds, kelvin, coulombs, joules --- and every angle is in
radians.  There is @emph{no unit tracking here}: these are numeric formulas, and
feeding one a value in grams or degrees silently gives an answer in the wrong
scale.  For dimensional work use the units functions below, which do track
units.

@example
escapevel(5.972e24, 6.371e6)                 @result{} 11185.9778919
lorentz(0.9)                                 @result{} 2.29415733871
veladd(0.5, 0.5)                             @result{} 0.8
massenergy(1)                                @result{} 89875517873681764
relke(1, 0.9)                                @result{} 1.16313061027e17
@end example

@strong{Relativity takes beta, not speed.}  The gamma-style functions take speed
as a @emph{fraction of the speed of light}: @samp{0.9} means 0.9c, not 0.9 m/s.
Results that are speeds (@code{veladd}) are likewise fractions of c; results
that are momenta or energies (@code{relmomentum}, @code{relke}) come back in SI.
Each docstring says which.

Symbolic algebra over these functions still composes --- a genuinely free
variable passes through unchecked:

@lisp
(cmacs-calculator-eval-symbolic "lorentz(b)")   @result{} "1 / sqrt(1 - b^2)"
@end lisp

@subsection Units and CODATA Constants

Calc exposes only @code{usimplify} as an algebraic function; converting to base
SI or to a named unit exists solely as interactive stack commands.  cmacs wraps
the underlying machinery so both are callable from Lisp:

@lisp
(cmacs-calculator-to-base-units "2 G * 1.989e30 kg / c^2")  ; @result{} "2954.12655505 m"
(cmacs-calculator-convert-units "100 km/hr" "m/s")          ; @result{} "27.7777777778"
(cmacs-calculator-convert-units "1 mi" "km")                ; @result{} "1.609344"
@end lisp

The first is the Schwarzschild radius of the Sun, in metres, from CODATA
@code{G} and @code{c}.

Note that Calc's @code{c}, @code{G} and @code{h} are entries in its @emph{units}
table, not numbers: @code{c} is the unit ``299792458 m/s'', not the integer.
They stay symbolic under ordinary evaluation and only resolve once something
converts units --- which is why the physics family carries its own private
constants rather than using them, and why unit expressions must go through these
two functions.

@subsection Computer Algebra (CAS)

@code{cmacs-calculator-eval-symbolic} permits free variables and does not force
a number, so algebra survives.  In a sheet, @kbd{C-c C-s} toggles symbolic mode.

@example
deriv(x^3 + sin(x), x)                       @result{} 3 x^2 + cos(x)
integ(x^2, x)                                @result{} x^3 / 3
solve(x^2 - 4 = 0, x)                        @result{} x = 2
taylor(sin(x), x, 5)                         @result{} x - x^3 / 6 + x^5 / 120
@end example

@node Calculator Catalog
@section Function Catalog

@cindex catalog
@cindex calculator functions

This is the complete reference to every named calculator, generated from the
registry that also backs the landing page, completion and the MCP tools, so it
cannot drift from the functions actually defined.  For the GNU Calc built-ins
these compose with, see @ref{Calculator Builtins}.  See @ref{Calculator
Semantics} for the corrected evaluation rules and @ref{Calculator Options} for
the surrounding machinery.

Every calculator is a first-class GNU Calc algebraic function (defined with
@code{cmacs-calculator-defcalc}), so it composes inside any larger expression
and works from a sheet, the REPL, inline @kbd{C-c =}, and @code{emacs --calc}.
Each example below is @emph{real output} from the engine.  Physics and
relativity arguments and results are in base SI units (metres, kilograms,
seconds, kelvin, coulombs, joules), and every angle is in radians; relativity
takes speed as @code{beta}, a fraction of the speed of light.

@subsection Financial

@table @code

@item accruedint(face, rate, days-since, days-period, freq)
Coupon interest accrued since the last payment, on a simple
day-count basis.

@table @code
@item face
Face value
@item rate
Annual coupon rate as a fraction
@item days-since
Days since the last coupon
@item days-period
Days in the full coupon period
@item freq
Coupon payments per year
@end table

Returns: Accrued interest

@example
accruedint(1000, 0.05, 90, 182, 2)  =>  12.3626373626
@end example

@item apr2apy(apr, periods)
Convert nominal annual rate APR, compounded PERIODS times per year,
into the effective annual yield.

@table @code
@item apr
Nominal annual rate as a fraction
@item periods
Compounding periods per year
@end table

Returns: Effective annual rate as a fraction

@example
apr2apy(0.05, 12)  =>  0.05116189792
@end example

@item apy2apr(apy, periods)
Convert effective annual yield APY into the nominal annual rate
compounded PERIODS times per year.

@table @code
@item apy
Effective annual rate as a fraction
@item periods
Compounding periods per year
@end table

Returns: Nominal annual rate as a fraction

@example
apy2apr(0.0511618978817, 12)  =>  0.05000000004
@end example

@item bondcurrentyield(face, rate, price)
Annual coupon income divided by the market PRICE.

@table @code
@item face
Face value
@item rate
Annual coupon rate as a fraction
