@subsection Physics

@table @code

@item angvel(freq)
Angular velocity of rotation at FREQ revolutions per second,
omega = 2 pi f.

@table @code
@item freq
Rotation frequency, Hz
@end table

Returns: Angular velocity, rad/s

@example
angvel(2)  =>  12.5663706144
@end example

@item bohrenergy(level)
Energy of the LEVEL-th state of hydrogen, E1 / n^2, where E1 is
-2.1798723611030e-18 J (-13.6056931230 eV).  Negative because the electron
is bound; divide by 1.602176634e-19 for electronvolts.  The energy of a
photon emitted in a transition is the difference of two of these.

@table @code
@item level
Principal quantum number n, a positive integer
@end table

Returns: Energy, J (negative)

@example
bohrenergy(1)  =>  -2.1798723611e-18
bohrenergy(2)  =>  -5.44968090275e-19
@end example

@item bohrradius(level)
Radius of the LEVEL-th orbit of hydrogen in the Bohr model,
n^2 a0.  LEVEL is the principal quantum number, 1 for the ground state.

@table @code
@item level
Principal quantum number n, a positive integer
@end table

Returns: Orbit radius, m

@example
bohrradius(1)  =>  5.29177210544e-11
bohrradius(2)  =>  2.11670884218e-10
@end example

@item capacitance(charge, volts)
Capacitance holding CHARGE at VOLTS, C = Q / V.

@table @code
@item charge
Stored charge, C
@item volts
Voltage across the plates, V
@end table

Returns: Capacitance, F

@example
capacitance(1e-6, 5)  =>  2e-7
@end example

@item capenergy(cap, volts)
Energy stored at VOLTS across capacitance CAP, C V^2 / 2.

@table @code
@item cap
Capacitance, F
@item volts
Voltage, V
@end table

Returns: Stored energy, J

@example
capenergy(1e-6, 12)  =>  7.2e-5
@end example

@item carnot(tcold, thot)
Maximum efficiency of any heat engine working between reservoirs at
THOT and TCOLD, 1 - Tc/Th.  Temperatures are absolute -- using Celsius here
is the classic error.  No real engine beats this.

@table @code
@item tcold
Cold reservoir temperature, K
@item thot
Hot reservoir temperature, K
@end table

Returns: Efficiency as a fraction, between 0 and 1

@example
carnot(300, 500)  =>  0.4
@end example

@item centripaccel(vel, radius)
Acceleration toward the centre for uniform circular motion at speed
VEL on a circle of RADIUS.

@table @code
@item vel
Tangential speed, m/s
@item radius
Radius of the circle, m
@end table

Returns: Centripetal acceleration, m/s^2

@example
centripaccel(10, 4)  =>  25
@end example

@item centripforce(mass, vel, radius)
Net inward force needed to keep MASS moving at VEL on a circle of
RADIUS.

@table @code
@item mass
Mass, kg
@item vel
Tangential speed, m/s
@item radius
Radius of the circle, m
@end table

Returns: Centripetal force, N

@example
centripforce(2, 10, 4)  =>  50
@end example

@item coulomb(q1, q2, dist)
Electrostatic force between point charges Q1 and Q2 separated by
DIST.  Positive means repulsion, negative attraction, so the sign of the
charges matters.

@table @code
@item q1
First charge, C
@item q2
Second charge, C
@item dist
Separation, m
@end table

Returns: Force, N (positive = repulsive)

@example
coulomb(1e-6, 1e-6, 0.1)  =>  0.89875517862
@end example

@item criticalangle(n1, n2)
Angle of incidence beyond which light travelling from index N1 into
index N2 is totally internally reflected, arcsin(n2/n1).  Exists only when
N1 exceeds N2; the call is rejected otherwise, since a ray leaving a rarer
medium never runs out of refraction angles.

@table @code
@item n1
Refractive index of the dense incident medium
@item n2
Refractive index of the rarer medium
@end table

Returns: Critical angle from the normal, radians

@example
criticalangle(1.33, 1)  =>  0.850908514478
@end example

@item debroglie(mass, vel)
Matter wavelength of a particle, lambda = h / (m v).  Non-relativistic;
above roughly 0.1c the momentum needs @code{relmomentum} instead.  The wavelength
of anything macroscopic is far too small to observe, which is why the
quantum world looks classical.

@table @code
@item mass
Mass, kg
@item vel
Speed, m/s
@end table

Returns: Wavelength, m

@example
debroglie(9.1093837139e-31, 1e6)  =>  7.27389509335e-10
@end example

@item dopplersound(freq, vsound, vobs, vsrc)
Frequency heard by a moving observer from a moving source,
f (v + vo) / (v - vs).

Signs follow the standard convention: VOBS is positive when the observer
moves *toward* the source, VSRC is positive when the source moves *toward*
the observer, so approaching raises the pitch either way.  These are speeds
through the medium, which is why the two enter asymmetrically -- unlike the
relativistic case, see @code{reldoppler}.

@table @code
@item freq
Emitted frequency, Hz
@item vsound
Speed of sound in the medium, m/s, about 343 in air
@item vobs
Observer speed toward the source, m/s
@item vsrc
Source speed toward the observer, m/s
@end table

Returns: Observed frequency, Hz

@example
dopplersound(440, 343, 0, 30)  =>  482.172523962
@end example

