Elapsed time, s
@end table

Returns: Velocity, m/s

@example
kinvel(0, 9.80665, 3)  =>  29.41995
@end example

@item kinvelpos(v0, accel, dist)
Speed reached after travelling DIST under constant ACCEL from V0.
The timeless kinematic equation v^2 = v0^2 + 2 a dx, solved for v -- use it
when the elapsed time is unknown.  Returns the positive root.

@table @code
@item v0
Initial velocity, m/s
@item accel
Constant acceleration, m/s^2
@item dist
Displacement, m
@end table

Returns: Speed, m/s

@example
kinvelpos(0, 9.80665, 10)  =>  14.0047491945
@end example

@item lensdi(focal, dobj)
Image distance for an object at DOBJ from a thin lens of focal
length FOCAL: from 1/f = 1/do + 1/di, so di = f do / (do - f).

Sign convention: FOCAL is positive for a converging lens and negative for a
diverging one; a positive result is a real image on the far side, a negative
one a virtual image on the object's side.  An object at the focal point
gives @code{uinf} -- the rays leave parallel and no image forms.

@table @code
@item focal
Focal length, m
@item dobj
Object distance from the lens, m
@end table

Returns: Image distance, m

@example
lensdi(0.1, 0.3)  =>  0.15
@end example

@item lensmag(dobj, dimg)
Magnification of a thin lens, -di/do.  Negative means the image is
inverted; a magnitude below 1 means it is smaller than the object.  Pair
with @code{lensdi}: lensmag(0.3, lensdi(0.1, 0.3)).

@table @code
@item dobj
Object distance, m
@item dimg
Image distance, m
@end table

Returns: Magnification (negative = inverted)

@example
lensmag(0.3, 0.15)  =>  -0.5
@end example

@item magforce(charge, vel, bfield, angle)
Lorentz magnetic force, q v B sin(theta), where ANGLE is between the
velocity and the field.  Zero when the charge moves along the field, maximal
across it.  This force is always perpendicular to the motion, so it does no
work and only bends the path.

@table @code
@item charge
Charge, C
@item vel
Speed, m/s
@item bfield
Magnetic flux density, T
@item angle
Angle between velocity and field, radians
@end table

Returns: Force, N

@example
magforce(1.602176634e-19, 1e6, 0.5, pi/2)  =>  8.01088317e-14
@end example

@item momentum(mass, vel)
Classical momentum, p = m v.  Above roughly 0.1c use @code{relmomentum}.

@table @code
@item mass
Mass, kg
@item vel
Velocity, m/s
@end table

Returns: Momentum, kg m/s

@example
momentum(2, 10)  =>  20
@end example

@item ohmi(volts, resist)
Current through a resistor, I = V / R.

@table @code
@item volts
Voltage, V
@item resist
Resistance, ohm
@end table

Returns: Current, A

@example
ohmi(110, 220)  =>  0.5
@end example

@item ohmr(volts, current)
Resistance implied by VOLTS and CURRENT, R = V / I.

@table @code
@item volts
Voltage, V
@item current
Current, A
@end table

Returns: Resistance, ohm

@example
ohmr(110, 0.5)  =>  220.
@end example

@item ohmv(current, resist)
Voltage across a resistor, V = I R.

@table @code
@item current
Current, A
@item resist
Resistance, ohm
@end table

Returns: Voltage, V

@example
ohmv(0.5, 220)  =>  110.
@end example

@item orbitalvel(mass, radius)
Speed needed for a circular orbit of RADIUS about a body of MASS,
sqrt(G M / r).  RADIUS is measured from the centre, not the surface.

@table @code
@item mass
Mass of the central body, kg
@item radius
Orbital radius from the centre, m
@end table

Returns: Orbital speed, m/s

@example
orbitalvel(5.972e24, 6.771e6)  =>  7672.49041328
@end example

@item orbitperiod(mass, semimajor)
Time for one orbit of semi-major axis SEMIMAJOR about a body of
MASS, 2 pi sqrt(a^3 / (G M)).  Kepler's third law solved for the period;
note it does not depend on the orbiting body's own mass.

@table @code
@item mass
Mass of the central body, kg
@item semimajor
Semi-major axis, m
@end table

Returns: Orbital period, s

@example
orbitperiod(1.989e30, 1.496e11)  =>  31554187.7477
@end example

@item pendulum(len)
Period of a simple pendulum of length LEN at Earth's surface,
2 pi sqrt(L/g).  The small-angle approximation: it does not depend on the
bob's mass, and holds to about 1% below roughly 20 degrees of swing.

@table @code
@item len
Pendulum length, m
@end table

Returns: Period, s

@example
pendulum(1)  =>  2.00640929259
@end example

@item photoelectric(freq, workfn)
Maximum kinetic energy of an electron ejected by light of frequency
FREQ from a surface of work function WORKFN: h f - phi.

A negative result means FREQ is below the threshold and no electron is
emitted at all -- the magnitude is how far short the photon falls.  Einstein's
point was that this depends on frequency, not intensity: brighter light below
the threshold still ejects nothing.  See @code{photothreshold}.  Work functions are
usually quoted in eV; multiply by 1.602176634e-19 for WORKFN.

@table @code
@item freq
Frequency of the incident light, Hz
@item workfn
Work function of the surface, J
@end table

Returns: Maximum electron kinetic energy, J (negative = below threshold)

@example
photoelectric(1e15, 7.22e-19)  =>  -5.9392985e-20
photoelectric(1.2e15, 7.22e-19)  =>  7.3128418e-20
@end example

@item photonenergy(freq)
Energy of a photon of frequency FREQ, E = h f.

@table @code
@item freq
Frequency, Hz
@end table

Returns: Photon energy, J

@example
photonenergy(6e14)  =>  3.97564209e-19
@end example

@item photonenergywl(wavelen)
Energy of a photon of wavelength WAVELEN, E = h c / lambda.  Visible
light runs from about 400 nm (violet) to 700 nm (red); divide the result by
1.602176634e-19 for electronvolts.

@table @code
@item wavelen
Wavelength, m
@end table

Returns: Photon energy, J

@example
photonenergywl(500e-9)  =>  3.9728917143e-19
@end example

@item photothreshold(workfn)
Lowest frequency that ejects an electron from a surface of work
function WORKFN, phi / h.  Below this @code{photoelectric} goes negative.

@table @code
@item workfn
Work function of the surface, J
@end table

Returns: Threshold frequency, Hz

@example
photothreshold(7.22e-19)  =>  1.0896353097e15
@end example

@item potential(mass, height)
Potential energy of MASS raised to HEIGHT near Earth's surface,
m g h.  Valid while HEIGHT is small against Earth's radius.

@table @code
@item mass
Mass, kg
@item height
Height above the reference level, m
@end table

Returns: Potential energy, J

@example
potential(2, 10)  =>  196.133
@end example

@item power(energy, secs)
Average rate of energy transfer, ENERGY over SECS.

@table @code
@item energy
Energy transferred, J
@item secs
Elapsed time, s
@end table

Returns: Power, W

@example
power(1000, 4)  =>  250
@end example

@item projheight(v0, angle)
Greatest height above the launch point reached by a projectile
launched at speed V0 and ANGLE above the horizontal.

@table @code
@item v0
Launch speed, m/s
@item angle
Launch angle above horizontal, radians
@end table

Returns: Maximum height, m

@example
projheight(20, pi/4)  =>  10.1971621298
@end example

@item projrange(v0, angle)
Horizontal distance travelled by a projectile launched at speed V0
and ANGLE above the horizontal, landing at its launch height.  Maximal at
ANGLE = pi/4.

@table @code
@item v0
Launch speed, m/s
@item angle
Launch angle above horizontal, radians
@end table

Returns: Range, m

@example
projrange(20, pi/4)  =>  40.7886485191
@end example

@item projtime(v0, angle)
Time a projectile launched at speed V0 and ANGLE above the horizontal
spends in the air before returning to its launch height.

@table @code
@item v0
Launch speed, m/s
@item angle
Launch angle above horizontal, radians
@end table

Returns: Time of flight, s

@example
projtime(20, pi/4)  =>  2.88419299633
@end example

@item respar(r1, r2)
Resistance of R1 and R2 in parallel, R1 R2 / (R1 + R2).  Always
smaller than either.  Nest for more than two:
respar(respar(100, 220), 330).

@table @code
@item r1
First resistance, ohm
@item r2
Second resistance, ohm
@end table

Returns: Total resistance, ohm

@example
respar(100, 220)  =>  68.75
@end example

@item resseries(r1, r2)
Resistance of R1 and R2 in series, R1 + R2.  Nest for more than
two: resseries(resseries(100, 220), 330).

@table @code
@item r1
First resistance, ohm
@item r2
Second resistance, ohm
@end table

Returns: Total resistance, ohm

@example
resseries(100, 220)  =>  320
@end example

@item rotke(inertia, omega)
Kinetic energy of a body with moment of INERTIA spinning at angular
velocity OMEGA, I w^2 / 2.  The rotational counterpart of @code{kinetic}.

@table @code
@item inertia
Moment of inertia, kg m^2
@item omega
Angular velocity, rad/s
@end table

Returns: Rotational kinetic energy, J

@example
rotke(0.25, 12.5663706144)  =>  19.7392088023
@end example

@item snell(n1, angle, n2)
Angle of the refracted ray crossing from a medium of index N1 into
one of index N2, arcsin(n1 sin(theta1) / n2).  Angles are from the normal,
in radians.

Rejected when the ray would be totally internally reflected -- going from
dense to rare beyond the critical angle, where no refracted ray exists.  See
@code{criticalangle}.

@table @code
@item n1
Refractive index of the incident medium, e.g. 1 for vacuum
@item angle
Angle of incidence from the normal, radians
@item n2
Refractive index of the transmitting medium, e.g. 1.5 for glass
@end table

Returns: Angle of refraction from the normal, radians

@example
snell(1, pi/6, 1.5)  =>  0.339836909454
@end example

@item springpe(springk, disp)
Energy stored in a spring of stiffness SPRINGK displaced by DISP
from equilibrium, k x^2 / 2.

@table @code
@item springk
Spring constant, N/m
@item disp
Displacement from equilibrium, m
@end table

Returns: Stored energy, J

@example
springpe(200, 0.1)  =>  1.
@end example

@item springperiod(mass, springk)
Period of a mass on a spring of stiffness SPRINGK,
2 pi sqrt(m/k).  Unlike @code{pendulum}, this is independent of gravity.

@table @code
@item mass
Mass, kg
@item springk
Spring constant, N/m
@end table

Returns: Period, s

@example
springperiod(0.5, 200)  =>  0.314159265359
@end example

@item thermexp(len, alpha, dtemp)
Change in length of an object of length LEN heated by DTEMP,
dL = L alpha dT.  ALPHA is about 1.2e-5 for steel and 2.3e-5 for aluminium.
Returns the change, not the new length.

@table @code
@item len
Original length, m
@item alpha
Coefficient of linear expansion, 1/K
@item dtemp
Temperature change, K
@end table

Returns: Change in length, m

@example
thermexp(10, 1.2e-5, 50)  =>  6e-3
@end example

@item torque(fmag, radius, angle)
Torque from a force of magnitude FMAG applied at RADIUS from the
axis, where ANGLE is between the lever arm and the force.  Maximal at
ANGLE = pi/2, zero for a force pointing along the arm.

@table @code
@item fmag
Force magnitude, N
@item radius
Distance from the axis, m
@item angle
Angle between lever arm and force, radians
@end table

Returns: Torque, N m

@example
torque(50, 0.3, pi/2)  =>  15.
@end example

@item visviva(mass, radius, semimajor)
Orbital speed at RADIUS on any conic orbit of semi-major axis
SEMIMAJOR about a body of MASS: sqrt(G M (2/r - 1/a)).  Reduces to
@code{orbitalvel} when RADIUS equals SEMIMAJOR (a circle), and to @code{escapevel} as
SEMIMAJOR tends to infinity (a parabola).

@table @code
@item mass
Mass of the central body, kg
@item radius
Current distance from the centre, m
@item semimajor
Semi-major axis of the orbit, m
@end table

Returns: Orbital speed, m/s

@example
visviva(1.989e30, 1.471e11, 1.496e11)  =>  30290.9383647
@end example

@item wavespeed(freq, wavelen)
Propagation speed of a wave, v = f lambda.

@table @code
@item freq
Frequency, Hz
@item wavelen
Wavelength, m
@end table

Returns: Wave speed, m/s

@example
wavespeed(440, 0.78)  =>  343.2
@end example

@item weight(mass)
Gravitational force on MASS at Earth's surface, using standard
gravity.  For another body use @code{gravforce}.

@table @code
@item mass
Mass, kg
@end table

Returns: Weight, N

@example
weight(70)  =>  686.4655
@end example

@item work(fmag, dist, angle)
Work done by a constant force of magnitude FMAG over DIST, where
ANGLE is between the force and the displacement.  Zero at ANGLE = pi/2, and
negative beyond it.

@table @code
@item fmag
Force magnitude, N
@item dist
Displacement, m
@item angle
Angle between force and displacement, radians
@end table

Returns: Work, J

@example
work(20, 5, 0)  =>  100
@end example

@end table

@subsection Relativity

@table @code

@item gravredshift(mass, radius)
Redshift z of light escaping to infinity from RADIUS above a body of
MASS, 1/sqrt(1 - rs/r) - 1.  The reciprocal of @code{gravtimedilation}, minus one:
light climbing out of a gravity well loses energy and reddens.  Diverges at
the horizon, where light cannot escape at all.

Rejected for RADIUS at or inside the horizon.

@table @code
@item mass
Mass of the gravitating body, kg
@item radius
Emission radius from the centre, m; must exceed the horizon
@end table

Returns: Redshift z, dimensionless

@example
gravredshift(5.972e24, 6.371e6)  =>  6.96107819392e-10
@end example

@item gravtimedilation(mass, radius)
Rate of a clock at RADIUS from a body of MASS, relative to one
infinitely far away: sqrt(1 - rs/r), where rs is the Schwarzschild radius.
Always below 1 -- clocks deeper in a gravity well run slow -- and it falls to
zero at the horizon.  The effect is tiny for ordinary bodies: about
0.9999999993 at Earth's surface, which GPS still has to correct for.

Rejected for RADIUS at or inside the horizon.  See @code{schwarzschild}.

@table @code
@item mass
Mass of the gravitating body, kg
@item radius
Distance from its centre, m; must exceed the horizon
@end table

Returns: Clock rate as a fraction of the far-away rate, below 1

@example
gravtimedilation(5.972e24, 6.371e6)  =>  0.999999999304
@end example

@item lengthcontraction(properlen, beta)
Length an observer measures for an object of rest length PROPERLEN
moving at BETA, L0 / gamma.  Contraction is along the direction of motion
only, and the result is always the smaller.

@table @code
@item properlen
Rest length of the object, m
@item beta
Speed as a fraction of c, |beta| < 1
@end table

Returns: Contracted length, m

@example
lengthcontraction(1, 0.9)  =>  0.435889894353
@end example

@item lorentz(beta)
The Lorentz factor gamma = 1 / sqrt(1 - beta^2), where BETA is speed
as a fraction of the speed of light: pass 0.9 for 0.9c.  Gamma is 1 at rest,
about 1.005 at 0.1c, and diverges as BETA approaches 1 -- which is why no
mass reaches c.

Rejected for |BETA| at or above 1; undefended the formula would return a
complex number.

@table @code
@item beta
Speed as a fraction of c, |beta| < 1
@end table

Returns: Lorentz factor gamma, always at least 1

@example
lorentz(0.9)  =>  2.29415733871
lorentz(0.99)  =>  7.08881205007
@end example

@item massenergy(mass)
Rest energy of MASS, E = m c^2.  One kilogram is about 9e16 J, some
21 megatons of TNT.

@table @code
@item mass
Rest mass, kg
@end table

Returns: Rest energy, J

@example
massenergy(1)  =>  89875517873681764
@end example

@item reldoppler(freq, beta)
Frequency observed from a source receding or approaching at BETA,
f sqrt((1 + beta) / (1 - beta)).  BETA is positive when the source
*approaches* (blueshift) and negative when it recedes (redshift).

Unlike @code{dopplersound}, only the relative speed enters -- there is no medium
to move through, so there is no separate source and observer term.

@table @code
@item freq
Emitted frequency, Hz
@item beta
Approach speed as a fraction of c, |beta| < 1;
negative when receding
@end table

Returns: Observed frequency, Hz

@example
reldoppler(440, 0.5)  =>  762.102355331
reldoppler(440, -0.5)  =>  254.034118443
@end example

@item relke(mass, beta)
Kinetic energy of MASS moving at BETA, (gamma - 1) m c^2.  MASS is
the rest mass in kg; the result is in joules.  Reduces to @code{kinetic} at low
speed and diverges as BETA approaches 1.

@table @code
@item mass
Rest mass, kg
@item beta
Speed as a fraction of c, |beta| < 1
@end table

Returns: Kinetic energy, J

@example
relke(1, 0.9)  =>  1.16313061027e17
relke(1, 1e-6)  =>  44937.7589369
@end example

@item relmomentum(mass, beta)
Momentum of MASS moving at BETA, gamma m v.  MASS is the rest mass
in kg and BETA a fraction of c, but the result is in SI units, kg m/s.
Exceeds the classical @code{momentum} by the factor gamma.

@table @code
@item mass
Rest mass, kg
@item beta
Speed as a fraction of c, |beta| < 1
@end table

Returns: Momentum, kg m/s

@example
relmomentum(1, 0.9)  =>  618993960.85
@end example

@item reltotale(mass, beta)
Total energy of MASS moving at BETA, gamma m c^2 -- rest energy plus
kinetic energy, so reltotale(m, b) equals massenergy(m) + relke(m, b).

@table @code
@item mass
Rest mass, kg
@item beta
Speed as a fraction of c, |beta| < 1
@end table

Returns: Total energy, J

@example
reltotale(1, 0.9)  =>  2.061885789e17
@end example

@item schwarzschild(mass)
Radius at which MASS becomes a black hole, 2 G M / c^2 -- the event
horizon of a non-rotating body.  About 2954 m for the Sun and 8.9 mm for the
Earth.

@table @code
@item mass
Mass, kg
@end table

Returns: Schwarzschild radius, m

@example
schwarzschild(1.989e30)  =>  2954.12655505
schwarzschild(5.972e24)  =>  8.86980582543e-3
@end example

@item timedilation(propertime, beta)
Time elapsed in the observer's frame while PROPERTIME passes on a
clock moving at BETA, gamma t0.  The moving clock runs slow, so the result
is always the larger.

@table @code
@item propertime
Time on the moving clock, s
@item beta
Speed as a fraction of c, |beta| < 1
@end table

Returns: Time in the observer's frame, s

@example
timedilation(1, 0.9)  =>  2.29415733871
@end example

@item twinage(earthtime, beta)
How much less the travelling twin ages than the stay-at-home twin
over EARTHTIME at speed BETA: t (1 - 1/gamma).  The traveller's own elapsed
time is EARTHTIME/gamma, so this is the difference between them.

There is no real paradox: the situation is not symmetric, because only the
traveller turns around and so changes inertial frame.  This assumes the
turnaround is brief against the trip.

@table @code
@item earthtime
Time elapsed for the stay-at-home twin, any time unit
@item beta
Travel speed as a fraction of c, |beta| < 1
@end table

Returns: Age difference, in the same unit as EARTHTIME

@example
twinage(10, 0.9)  =>  5.64110105648
twinage(10, 1e-6)  =>  5e-12
@end example

@item veladd(beta1, beta2)
Composition of two collinear velocities, (b1 + b2) / (1 + b1 b2),
with both given and returned as fractions of c.  The result never reaches 1
however close the inputs get -- veladd(0.9, 0.9) is 0.994, not 1.8 -- and
veladd(1, x) would be exactly 1, which is light speed being the same in
every frame.

@table @code
@item beta1
First speed as a fraction of c, |beta| < 1
@item beta2
Second speed as a fraction of c, |beta| < 1
@end table

Returns: Combined speed as a fraction of c

@example
veladd(0.9, 0.9)  =>  0.994475138122
veladd(0.5, 0.5)  =>  0.8
@end example

@end table

@subsection Tax

@table @code

@item aftertax(income, tax)
Income remaining once TAX is paid.

@table @code
@item income
Gross income
@item tax
Tax owed
@end table

Returns: After-tax income

@example
aftertax(100000, 17000)  =>  83000
@end example

@item effrate(tax, income)
TAX as a fraction of INCOME -- the average rate actually paid,
as opposed to the marginal rate on the last dollar.

@table @code
@item tax
Tax owed
@item income
Income the tax was computed on
@end table

Returns: Effective rate as a fraction, e.g. 0.17 for 17%

@example
effrate(17000, 100000)  =>  0.17
@end example

@item ficatax(wages, rate, cap)
Tax at RATE on WAGES up to CAP, and nothing above it.  The shape
of the Social Security tax: once wages pass the cap the levy stops, so
this flattens out rather than growing.

@table @code
@item wages
Wages subject to the tax
@item rate
Rate as a fraction, e.g. 0.062
@item cap
Wage cap above which no further tax is due
@end table

Returns: Tax owed

@example
ficatax(200000, 0.062, 184500)  =>  11439.
@end example

@item flattax(income, rate, deduction)
Tax at a single RATE on INCOME above DEDUCTION.  The structure
used by the flat-rate states; the deduction floor means a low income
owes zero rather than a negative tax.

@table @code
@item income
Gross income
@item rate
Flat rate as a fraction, e.g. 0.0425
@item deduction
Amount exempt from tax
@end table

Returns: Tax owed

@example
flattax(100000, 0.0425, 15000)  =>  3612.5
@end example

@item grossup(net, rate)
Gross amount needed to leave NET after tax at RATE.  Note this is
the inverse of a FLAT rate: grossing up through a progressive ladder
needs @code{cmacs-calculator-tax-federal}, since the rate itself moves.

@table @code
@item net
Desired after-tax amount
@item rate
Tax rate as a fraction
@end table

Returns: Required gross amount

@example
grossup(1000, 0.22)  =>  1282.05128205
@end example

@end table

@include calculator/builtins.texi

@include calculator/constants.texi

@node Calculator Charting
@section Charting

@cindex chart
@cindex plot

Two tiers, chosen by @code{cmacs-calculator-chart-backend}.  The default
@code{auto} degrades SVG @result{} unicode as capabilities disappear, so a chart
request @emph{never hard-fails} just because a build lacks GL.

Both tiers consume the same input --- a list of series plists mirroring
libregnum's @code{LrgChartDataSeries} / @code{LrgChartDataPoint}:

@lisp
(:name "Principal" :points ((0 . 1000) (1 . 950) ...) :color "#3b6fb0")
@end lisp

@subsection Tier 1: SVG (Default)

Renders inline next to the formula that produced it, needs no GL and no
libregnum, and is testable without a display.  Amortization curves, option
payoff diagrams and function plots land here unless something better is asked
for.  Where SVG is unavailable --- a TTY, batch --- @code{auto} falls back to a
@strong{unicode approximation}, which is why plotting works over SSH and in
tests.  Real output from @code{emacs -Q --batch}:

@example
sin          ▄▄▅▅▅▆▆▆▇▇▇▇▇▇▇█▇▇▇▇▇▇▇▆▆▆▅▅▅▄▄▃▃▃▂▂▂▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▂▂▂▃▃▃▄▄  [-0.9996 .. 0.9996]
@end example

Sample a function into series points with @code{cmacs-calculator-chart-sample}:

@lisp
(cmacs-calculator-chart-sample "sin(x)" "x" 0 6.283 5)
;; @result{} ((0.0 . 0.0) (1.57075 . 0.999999998927) (3.1415 . 9.26535896674e-05)
;;    (4.71225 . -0.999999990342) (6.283 . -0.000185307178539))
@end lisp

@subsection Tier 2: libregnum (GPU), Opt-In

libregnum ships a full 2D chart module rendered on the GPU into an Emacs buffer,
with axes, legends, tooltips, animation and hit-testing.  It needs
@option{--with-cmacs-libregnum} @emph{and a display}, and it is @emph{not a
drop-in} for the inline tiers: @code{cmacs-calculator-chart-open} opens a buffer
rather than returning an insertable string.

Types, from @code{(cmacs-calculator-chart-types)}:

@example
line  bar  area  scatter  pie  candlestick  histogram  radar  gauge  heatmap
@end example

@noindent
so candlestick, heatmap, radar and gauge are Tier 2 only.

@strong{Tier 2 renders identically under pgtk and @code{emacs --lrg}.}  That
falls out of the design rather than being maintained twice: charts are
@code{LrgWidget}s drawn with immediate-mode @code{grl_draw_*} calls, which are
only legal inside the render bracket, so the draw lives in the libregnum
renderer's chart branch and both display backends reach it by the same path.

@strong{2D only.}  libregnum's 3D chart types (@code{LineChart3D},
@code{PieChart3D}, @code{SurfaceChart3D}, @code{ScatterChart3D}) are
@emph{unimplemented} --- its own @file{ROADMAP.md} lists them as missing, while
the 2D chart system exists and is tested.  There is therefore no 3D charting
here, and nothing in cmacs can add it until libregnum does.

@node Calculator Tax
@section Tax: Scope and Caveats

@quotation Important
@strong{This is informational only and is not tax advice.}  It is a calculator,
not a tax preparer.  Do not file, withhold, or make a financial decision on
these figures.  Verify every number against primary sources or a qualified
professional before relying on it.
@end quotation

@subsection Data-Driven and Versioned

Not one line of arithmetic knows a tax rate.  Every bracket, cap and deduction
lives in @file{cmacs-calculator-tax-data.el} as a versioned plist; the math in
@file{cmacs-calculator-tax.el} is year- and jurisdiction-agnostic.  A new tax
year is a @emph{data edit and never a code change}, and the same bracket walk
that handles the US federal ladder handles a state one --- or a German one, if
someone registered it.  52 jurisdictions are registered: US federal plus all
fifty states and the District of Columbia.

@lisp
(cmacs-calculator-tax-federal 100000 'single)
;; @result{} (:jurisdiction us-federal :year 2026 :status single :gross-income 100000
;;    :standard-deduction 16100 :taxable-income 83900 :tax 13170.0
;;    :marginal-rate 0.22 :effective-rate 0.1317 :verified t
;;    :source "IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-32, ...")

(cmacs-calculator-tax-fica 100000 'single)
;; @result{} (... :social-security 6200.0 :medicare 1450.0 :additional-medicare 0.0
;;    :total 7650.0 :effective-rate 0.0765 :wage-cap 184500 :verified t ...)

(cmacs-calculator-tax-state 100000 'single 'us-ca)
;; @result{} (:jurisdiction us-ca :year 2025 :taxable-income 94294 :tax 5207.98
;;    :marginal-rate 0.093 ... :verified t :source "California FTB, ...")
@end lisp

Every result carries its own @code{:verified} flag and @code{:source} --- the
answer tells you where it came from.

@subsection Brackets Are Marginal

@code{cmacs-calculator-tax-bracket-liability} is the core, and the one place a
bug would be both easy and expensive.  A 22% bracket starting at $50,400 does
@emph{not} mean someone earning $50,401 owes 22% of $50,401 --- it means 22% of
the @emph{one dollar} above $50,400, on top of the tax on the first $50,400.
Getting this wrong is the classic tax-calculator bug (``a raise pushed me into a
higher bracket and I take home less''), and it is wrong by thousands of dollars.

The walk sums each band separately, and is checked against the IRS's own
published cumulative figures --- computed by the IRS, not by this code.  The
2026 single ladder reproduces Rev. Proc. 2025-32's ``The Tax Is'' column
exactly:

@multitable @columnfractions .5 .5
@headitem Taxable income @tab Liability
@item @samp{12400}  @tab @samp{1240.0}
@item @samp{50400}  @tab @samp{5800.0}
@item @samp{105700} @tab @samp{17966.0}
@item @samp{201775} @tab @samp{41024.0}
@item @samp{256225} @tab @samp{58448.0}
@item @samp{640600} @tab @samp{192979.25}
@end multitable

Long-term capital gains stack @emph{on top of} ordinary income, which is exactly
a bracket walk run twice --- @code{tax(ordinary + gain) - tax(ordinary)} over the
capital-gain ladder --- so the 0/15/20 thresholds stay ordinary data.

@subsection Trust, but Report

Wrong tax data is worse than no tax data, because it looks like an answer.
Every table carries @code{:year}, @code{:source}, @code{:retrieved} and
@code{:verified}:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{:verified t} is a promise about @emph{provenance} --- these figures came
from that named document, for that year.  It is @emph{not} a promise that the
computed figure is anyone's true liability.
@item
Tables that could not be fully confirmed are registered @code{:verified nil}
with a @code{:note} saying precisely what is missing --- an honest gap rather
than a plausible invention.  @strong{12 of the registered tables are currently
unverified.}
@item
@code{:year} is the year the figures actually apply to.  A state that has
published only 2025 figures is recorded as @code{:year 2025}, never relabelled
to look current --- note the California example above is a 2025 table.
@item
Nothing is inferred: a bracket that could not be read from a source is not
guessed from last year, a neighbouring state, or an aggregator's summary.
@end itemize

@code{(cmacs-calculator-tax-vintage 'us-federal)} dates a table (@code{:age},
@code{:stale}, @code{:verified}, @code{:source}, @code{:note}), and
@code{(cmacs-calculator-tax-validate)} checks every table's shape --- ascending
brackets, rates in [0,1], required keys, and the @code{:verified nil} @result{}
@code{:note} pairing.  It returns @code{nil} when everything is well-formed.

@strong{Check the tables against primary sources before relying on them.}
Vintage and validation tell you how old a table is and whether it is
well-formed; neither can tell you the law did not change.

@subsection Deliberately NOT Modeled

This is the honest boundary of the tax family.  It does @emph{not} model:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Municipal and local income taxes} (only flagged via @code{:local-tax}).
@item
@strong{Reciprocity agreements} between states.
@item
@strong{Credits} beyond the standard deduction --- EITC, CTC, and the rest.
@item
Itemized deductions, the AMT, QBI, phase-outs, and the 3.8% net investment
income tax.
@end itemize

Results are estimates of the @emph{modeled components only}, and the paycheck
breakdown is @emph{not} a substitute for a W-4 withholding calculation.

